WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.347
If you have ever rendered with a path tracer, you have met fireflies.

2
00:00:04.697 --> 00:00:11.822
Single pixels that blaze white, scattered at random, that refuse to go away no matter how long you wait.

3
00:00:12.172 --> 00:00:17.889
They love one situation most. A small, bright light, reflected in something glossy.

4
00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:25.066
Here is a glaze tile in front of a glowing kiln mouth. The middle of the reflection is peppered with bright specks.

5
00:00:25.416 --> 00:00:32.947
Where do these specks come from, and how do renderers make them disappear? The answer is a tiny formula with a big idea.

6
00:00:34.147 --> 00:00:41.848
A renderer estimates light by guessing. It picks random directions, asks how much light arrives from each, and averages.

7
00:00:42.198 --> 00:00:48.747
But it does not pick directions evenly. Each strategy has a probability for every direction, called p.

8
00:00:49.097 --> 00:00:55.455
To stay fair, each sample is divided by its own probability. Value f, divided by p.

9
00:00:55.805 --> 00:01:04.935
Usually that is harmless. But if a direction was very unlikely to be picked, and it turns out to be very bright, then f over p is enormous.

10
00:01:05.285 --> 00:01:10.064
One giant number in the average. One white pixel. That is a firefly.

11
00:01:11.264 --> 00:01:15.211
There are two natural ways to pick directions for a shiny surface.

12
00:01:15.561 --> 00:01:23.497
The first follows the material. It sends samples where the glossy reflection points. This is called B S D F sampling.

13
00:01:23.847 --> 00:01:30.716
The second follows the light. It picks a random point on the kiln mouth and aims at it. This is light sampling.

14
00:01:31.066 --> 00:01:35.525
Both are perfectly correct on average. They just fail in different places.

15
00:01:36.725 --> 00:01:44.213
On the highlight, which we call the lip, the glossy lobe is narrow, about zero point zero three nine steradians.

16
00:01:44.563 --> 00:01:55.357
The kiln mouth looks about eight times bigger, at zero point three two steradians. So light sampling mostly lands outside the lobe, and only rarely inside it.

17
00:01:55.707 --> 00:02:01.126
Those rare hits have a small light probability and a huge glossy value. Fireflies.

18
00:02:01.476 --> 00:02:10.948
Away from the highlight, at the point we call the miss, it flips. Glossy samples rarely hit the light at all, and the material strategy turns grainy.

19
00:02:11.298 --> 00:02:21.133
So here is the aha. A firefly is not random bad luck. It is a direction that one strategy loves, sampled by the strategy that ignores it.

20
00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:28.093
So why not use both strategies, and let each sample ask both of them how likely it was?

21
00:02:28.443 --> 00:02:36.528
That is the balance heuristic. The weight for strategy i is its own probability, divided by the sum of both probabilities.

22
00:02:36.878 --> 00:02:43.619
Multiply it through, and each sample contributes f, divided by p B S D F plus p light.

23
00:02:43.969 --> 00:02:52.247
Now look at that denominator. If either strategy likes this direction, the denominator is big, so the result cannot explode.

24
00:02:52.597 --> 00:03:07.125
Right on the reflection at the lip, p B S D F is eleven point six six, and p light is zero point nine six. The material sample keeps about ninety-two percent of the weight. The light sample gets about eight.

25
00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:14.021
Now the measurement. These maps show noise on the tile, at sixty-four samples per pixel.

26
00:03:14.371 --> 00:03:21.795
Left, material sampling. It is noisy away from the highlight. Middle, light sampling. It is hot right on the highlight.

27
00:03:22.145 --> 00:03:24.577
Right, balance. Quiet in both places.

28
00:03:24.927 --> 00:03:36.063
On the lip, the average noise is zero point three zero eight for light sampling, zero point one two zero for the material, and zero point one three one for balance.

29
00:03:36.413 --> 00:03:46.802
At the miss, the material is at zero point zero seven seven, light sampling at zero point zero one three, and balance at zero point zero one eight.

30
00:03:48.002 --> 00:03:53.485
Here is the error as the sample count grows, from sixteen to one thousand twenty-four.

31
00:03:53.835 --> 00:04:08.043
At the lip, light sampling starts at zero point seven three and ends at zero point zero seven eight. Balance goes from zero point two seven to zero point zero three nine, just above the material.

32
00:04:08.393 --> 00:04:16.777
At the miss, material sampling starts above one. Balance ends at zero point zero three four, just above light sampling.

33
00:04:17.127 --> 00:04:22.289
Every curve falls. Balance is never the very best, but it is never the disaster.

34
00:04:23.489 --> 00:04:24.983
Now the honest part.

35
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:32.885
At each probe, the winning single strategy still beats balance by a little. Balance buys safety, not perfection.

36
00:04:33.235 --> 00:04:42.728
This is one kiln scene, one equal split of samples, on a software renderer. Other weighting rules, like the power heuristic, were not measured here.

37
00:04:43.078 --> 00:04:51.569
The pictures are for looking. Every number comes from the estimator itself. The run finishes with fifty-three checks passed and none failed.

38
00:04:52.769 --> 00:04:54.113
So, the takeaway.

39
00:04:54.463 --> 00:05:03.167
Fireflies come from dividing by a tiny probability. Ask every strategy for its probability, and the denominator stays big.

40
00:05:03.517 --> 00:05:10.663
This idea, multiple importance sampling, is at the heart of essentially every modern production path tracer.

41
00:05:11.013 --> 00:05:16.731
It comes from Eric Veach and Leonidas Guibas, nineteen ninety-five. Thanks for watching.
